<p>While I understand and sympathize with the desire to employ hyperbole regarding the SCP Series, I feel I must caution my colleagues against it. We should endeavor at all times to maintain scientific skepticism. For that reason, I strongly object to stating that SCP-073's presence makes land "<em>permanently</em> unable to support life. [emphasis mine]". We cannot say that with any certainty; it is beyond our capability to confirm. Rather, we should state that the soil has remained unable to support life <em>for as far back as our records go</em>. I furthermore urge continued testing to determine any possible ways to reverse the effect. We don't want to be caught with our pants down if a similar effect is ever weaponized and used against us! There are a number of SCPs that have not been terminated simply because we fear encountering similar things in future, and we wish to be prepared for any eventuality. I propose a land reclamation project (perhaps using SCPs, sparingly and under controlled experimental conditions. SCP-124 + SCP-914, anyone?) to determine the full extent of SCP-073's ability to make land infertile.</p>

							</div>

								
